---
title: Buttery pie crust
servings: 2
yield: 2 single-crust or 1 double-crust 9-inch pie
tags: [holiday, make-ahead]
cuisine: american
time: 3 hours 20 minutes
source: Buttery, Flaky Pie Crust (Old-Fashioned Flaky Pie Dough) by Stella Parks, Serious Eats, https://www.seriouseats.com/old-fashioned-flaky-pie-dough-recipe
nutrition:
  calories: 125 kcal
---

== Dough ==

Whisk the @all-purpose flour{225%g}, @granulated sugar{15%g} and @salt{4%g} in a #medium bowl{}.

Cut the @unsalted butter{225%g} into cubes of at least 1/2 inch and toss them in the flour. Press each cube flat between your fingertips; no rubbing in.

Stir in the @water{115%g} and knead against the side of the bowl into a shaggy ball. It should be 65 to 70°F (18 to 21°C); chill briefly if warmer.

On a well-floured counter, roll it to about 10 by 15 inches. Fold the short ends to the middle, close it like a book, then fold in half again into a thick block. Cut it in two. Check it is still 65 to 70°F before going on.

== Single-crust pies ==

Roll one piece into a 14-inch round and lay it in a #9-inch pie plate{}. Brush off extra flour and ease the dough into the corners.

Trim to a 1 1/4-inch overhang, fold the overhang under so the thick rim sits on top of the plate's edge, and crimp. Repeat with the other piece.

Wrap and chill at least ~{2%hours}, or overnight.

== Double-crust pie ==

Line the plate with one piece as above, trimmed to a 1 1/4-inch overhang. Roll the other piece to a 14-inch round for a solid top, or a 9 by 15-inch sheet for a lattice, and lay it on a parchment-lined board.

Wrap both and chill at least ~{2%hours}, or overnight. Once filled and sealed, chill the pie ~{30%minutes} before baking.

== Blind baking ==

Heat the oven to 350°F (180°C) with a rack in the lower-middle. Line a chilled shell with foil pressed into the curves and fill it to the brim with @granulated sugar{}(as pie weights).

Set it on a #half sheet pan{} and bake ~{60%minutes} to 75 minutes, until set and golden at the edges.

Gather the foil and lift the sugar out into a heatproof bowl to cool; it can be reused. Bake the crust a few minutes longer if the bottom needs colour.
